  "overview": {
    "text": "RNA, or ribonucleic acid, is a nucleic acid that helps cells use and regulate genetic information. It is made by copying DNA in transcription and is often—but not always—single-stranded; its four bases are adenine, uracil, cytosine, and guanine. RNA is not just a temporary message: messenger RNA (mRNA) carries protein-making instructions to ribosomes, transfer RNA (tRNA) delivers amino acids, and ribosomal RNA (rRNA) helps form ribosomes and catalyze protein assembly. Other RNAs regulate gene activity; for example, small interfering RNAs can guide the breakdown of matching mRNA, reducing protein production. This variety makes RNA central to gene expression and control.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/educational-resources/fact-sheets/ribonucleic-acid-fact-sheet",
      "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68034622"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "RNA is often single-stranded, but some RNA molecules are not; RNA contains the bases A, U, C, and G.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/educational-resources/fact-sheets/ribonucleic-acid-fact-sheet"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Ribosomes read mRNA in three-nucleotide units called codons to help assemble proteins.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/educational-resources/fact-sheets/ribonucleic-acid-fact-sheet"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "tRNA folds into a distinctive L-shape; one end matches an mRNA codon, while another carries an amino acid.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/educational-resources/fact-sheets/ribonucleic-acid-fact-sheet"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In RNA interference, small interfering RNA (siRNA) guides a complex to matching mRNA, which can then be cleaved and degraded.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68034622"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Other gene-regulating RNAs include microRNAs, which can block protein production, and long noncoding RNAs, which have varied regulatory roles.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/understanding/basics/noncodingdna/"
      ]
    }
  ],
